(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cardiomyopathy-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Cardiomyopathy Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 20-year-old man with acute myocarditis after a flulike illness is on the ward with heart failure symptoms. He reports new palpitations, and his nurse notes a brief episode of lightheadedness. Which complication poses the most immediate threat to his life?","choices":["Aortic dissection","Pericardial thickening","Mitral valve prolapse","Ventricular arrhythmia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Palpitations, syncope or sudden cardiac death in myocarditis are due to underlying ventricular arrhythmias or atrioventricular block, and myocarditis can cause rapidly progressive, often fatal, arrhythmia. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with chest pain and breathlessness after a recent viral illness is being evaluated for myocarditis. Her electrocardiogram shows sinus tachycardia. Which laboratory result would indicate cardiac myonecrosis?","choices":["Elevated serum troponin","A low white cell count","Normal sedimentation rate","A low natriuretic peptide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Serum troponin is the indicator of cardiac myonecrosis in myocarditis. The rest of the work-up adds leukocytosis on the blood count, a possibly elevated sedimentation rate and electrocardiogram changes. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman admitted with stress cardiomyopathy after a house fire has a repeat echocardiogram that shows a thrombus in the akinetic left ventricular apex. She is in sinus rhythm. Which treatment is now indicated?","choices":["Nitrate infusion","Surgical myectomy","Anticoagulation","Pacemaker insertion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of Takotsubo is empiric and supportive and depends on the presentation: anticoagulation if a left ventricular thrombus is present, beta blockers as helpful, and diuretics if heart failure is present. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy has taken a beta blocker, an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, spironolactone and a diuretic for many months. She still has breathlessness on moderate exertion, and a repeat echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 25%. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Implantable cardioverter defibrillator","Endomyocardial biopsy to find a cause","Surgical myectomy of the septum","Stopping the beta blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An implantable cardioverter defibrillator is indicated when the ejection fraction stays below 35% after maximum medical therapy, because sudden cardiac death from arrhythmia is a major cause of death in dilated cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy and marked biatrial enlargement has had palpitations for a week, and the electrocardiogram now shows atrial fibrillation. Which treatment reduces her risk of embolism?","choices":["Surgical myectomy","A positive inotrope","Anticoagulation","Aggressive diuresis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is common in restrictive cardiomyopathy because of atrial enlargement, it raises the risk of atrial thrombus, and anticoagulants may be needed to prevent embolism from atrial thrombi. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man with suspected viral myocarditis has an elevated troponin and normal coronary arteries. His cardiologist wants to see how much of the heart muscle is inflamed without an invasive procedure. Which test shows the extent of inflammation?","choices":["Gadolinium-enhanced cardiac magnetic resonance imaging","Ambulatory rhythm monitoring for forty-eight hours","Exercise echocardiography with treadmill testing","Right heart catheterization with pressure tracings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gadolinium-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging is part of the myocarditis work-up because the gadolinium improves visibility of the inflammation and shows its extent. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman was admitted with chest pain after a near drowning. Her electrocardiogram showed ST elevation with T-wave inversion, and echocardiography showed hypokinesis of the midsegment and apex of the left ventricle. Which catheterization finding is expected if this is stress cardiomyopathy?","choices":["A proximal coronary occlusion","A ruptured coronary plaque","Triple-vessel narrowing","Normal coronary arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stress cardiomyopathy mimics acute coronary syndrome but lacks angiographic coronary artery disease or acute plaque rupture; catheterization most often shows normal coronary arteries. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is admitted after surgery with low blood pressure, and a resident proposes a drug to strengthen her heart's contraction. Which drug class should be avoided in her?","choices":["Beta blockers such as metoprolol","Antiarrhythmics such as amiodarone","Calcium channel blockers like verapamil","Positive inotropes such as digoxin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Positive inotropes such as digoxin, epinephrine and norepinephrine are avoided because the increased contractility worsens outflow obstruction, raises the risk of myocardial ischemia and may induce arrhythmias. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman with a week of fever and myalgia now has severe breathlessness. She is cold and clammy with a blood pressure of 78/50, heart rate 128, raised jugular venous pressure and crackles throughout both lungs. Myocarditis is suspected. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Exercise testing to grade severity","Aggressive hemodynamic stabilization","Outpatient follow-up in one week","Elective biopsy before any support"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Myocarditis can present as cardiogenic shock, and although supportive care is the mainstay, treatment is directed toward the scenario and may require aggressive hemodynamic stabilization first. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and outflow tract obstruction has breathlessness and chest tightness on exertion. His blood pressure is 124/78 and heart rate is 76. He takes no cardiac medication yet. Which is the initial medication for his outflow obstruction?","choices":["A high-intensity loop diuretic","A beta blocker such as metoprolol","A nitrate such as nitroglycerin","A positive inotrope such as digoxin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A beta blocker such as metoprolol is the initial medication for left ventricular outflow tract obstruction; it slows the heart and improves diastolic filling. Verapamil or diltiazem is the alternative. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with restrictive cardiomyopathy from cardiac sarcoidosis has two episodes of syncope. His heart rate is 34, and the electrocardiogram shows complete heart block. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Surgical myectomy","Alcohol septal ablation","Endomyocardial biopsy","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fibrosis of the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes in restrictive cardiomyopathy can lead to heart block, and a permanent pacemaker is used for this conduction system disease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has just been diagnosed with symptomatic idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 25%. He asks what his long-term outlook is. Which statement about his prognosis is most accurate?","choices":["Nearly all recover fully in weeks","Worst outlook of any heart muscle disease","A normal life span without therapy","About half die within five years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Once symptoms emerge, prognosis is poor overall, with 50% mortality at five years, largely from progressive disease and sudden cardiac death due to arrhythmia. The course depends on response to therapy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with stable cardiomyopathy on full medical therapy says he tires after walking a block and feels anxious about exercising. He has no symptoms at rest. Which referral is most appropriate to improve his exercise tolerance?","choices":["A competitive sports clearance clinic","A cardiac rehabilitation program","Hospice referral for end-stage care","Genetic screening of his children"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac rehabilitation programs improve exercise tolerance and quality of life, with education on medication adherence, nutrition and symptom monitoring, and psychological support for chronic illness. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy has fatigue and exercise intolerance along with leg edema, hepatomegaly and ascites, but her lungs are relatively clear. Which pattern of heart failure is expected to dominate in this condition?","choices":["Isolated left-sided failure","High-output heart failure","Pure systolic failure","Right-sided heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Although filling pressures are raised in both ventricles, right heart failure is often more prominent and may dominate in restrictive cardiomyopathy, giving edema, hepatomegaly and ascites. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 17-year-old boy with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy asks his clinician what the most serious risk of his disease is. He has occasional palpitations and plays recreational basketball. Which complication is the leading cause of death in this condition?","choices":["Embolic stroke","Aortic dissection","Sudden cardiac death","Progressive heart failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sudden cardiac death is the leading cause of mortality in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, highest in preadolescents and adolescents with extreme exertion, and over 80% of cases are associated with ventricular fibrillation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with suspected restrictive cardiomyopathy has right-sided heart failure and big atria on echocardiogram. Her cardiologist wants to measure pressures inside both ventricles to characterize the filling problem. Which invasive test evaluates ventricular pressure tracings?","choices":["Exercise echocardiography","Cardiac catheterization","Endomyocardial biopsy","Transesophageal echocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac catheterization is useful in restrictive cardiomyopathy to evaluate ventricular pressure tracings, alongside cardiac magnetic resonance imaging and, when needed, endomyocardial biopsy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy has orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat), crackles at both lung bases and diminished breath sounds at the right base. A chest radiograph is ordered. Which finding on the film would indicate pulmonary edema?","choices":["Short horizontal lines meeting the pleura","A small heart with clear lung fields","A normal film that excludes congestion","Isolated thickening of the pericardium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Kerley B lines are horizontal lines 1 to 2 cm long that meet the pleura at right angles and indicate pulmonary edema. Cardiomegaly and pleural effusions are the other expected radiograph findings. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with new-onset heart failure a week after a flulike illness. He smokes and has hyperlipidemia, so the team wants to exclude coronary disease before labeling this myocarditis. Which test rules out coronary ischemia as the cause of his new heart failure?","choices":["Sedimentation rate","Chest radiograph","Cardiac angiography","Endomyocardial biopsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac angiography is used in the myocarditis work-up to rule out coronary ischemia as the cause of new-onset heart failure, which matters in a patient with coronary risk factors. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has restrictive cardiomyopathy confirmed on imaging, but cardiac magnetic resonance imaging and genetic testing have not identified a cause. His team is considering endomyocardial biopsy. When is biopsy performed in this condition?","choices":["Only after cardiac transplantation is done","Never, since it cannot change management","When imaging and genetic tests are negative","As the first test in every suspected case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Endomyocardial biopsy is performed when all imaging studies and genetic testing are negative, or when a tissue sample is likely to confirm or exclude a diagnosis that would change management. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has new dilated cardiomyopathy with an ejection fraction of 30%. He reports fatigue, heat intolerance and weight loss, and the conjunctivae look pale. Which initial laboratory tests are most useful for finding a contributing cause?","choices":["Blood cultures and a throat swab","Urine osmolality and cortisol","Thyroid function and a blood count","Serum lipase and amylase levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The initial laboratory work-up for dilated cardiomyopathy includes a complete blood count for anemia, a metabolic panel, liver function and thyroid function. His symptoms point to thyroid disease and anemia. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is leaving for a summer hiking trip in a hot climate. She takes a beta blocker and asks how to lower her risk of symptoms while away. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Take extra diuretic on hot days","Use alcohol to relax at the campsite","Stay well hydrated; avoid dehydration","Restrict fluids to soften the murmur"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy should maintain hydration, because dehydration lowers preload and worsens outflow obstruction; diuretics are used with caution for the same reason. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman has just been diagnosed with restrictive cardiomyopathy from amyloidosis. She has right-sided heart failure and asks how her condition compares with other kinds of heart muscle disease. Which statement about her prognosis is most accurate?","choices":["Poorest of all heart muscle diseases","Complete recovery within weeks","A normal life span with diuretics","Better than other cardiomyopathies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Restrictive cardiomyopathy has the poorest prognosis among all types of heart muscle disease, and it often presents at an advanced stage with pronounced cardiopulmonary symptoms. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has right-sided heart failure with a raised jugular venous pressure and ascites. Echocardiogram shows abnormal diastolic filling, normal-sized ventricles and big atria, but the team cannot tell restrictive cardiomyopathy from constrictive pericarditis. Which test is used to differentiate them?","choices":["Ambulatory rhythm monitoring","Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging","Repeat transthoracic echocardiogram","Exercise stress echocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiography alone is not definitive because restrictive cardiomyopathy and constrictive pericarditis look alike; cardiac magnetic resonance imaging differentiates them by showing pericardial thickening in pericarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy is seen for a sports physical. He has no symptoms, but his father was recently diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, which is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. Which strategy for this adolescent is most appropriate?","choices":["Genetic screening for the condition","Waiting until adulthood to evaluate","A routine chest radiograph each year","An endomyocardial biopsy at this visit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age-specific strategies for adolescents include genetic screening for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and disease detected in a family member is a common reason evaluation begins. One affected parent is enough to pass it on. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has just undergone alcohol septal ablation, in which alcohol is injected through a catheter into a septal branch of the coronary artery. He asks when he can expect his symptoms to improve. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Never; it is a diagnostic test","Over months as the septum thins","Within minutes of the injection","Only after a repeat injection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alcohol septal ablation induces a therapeutic infarction of the proximal septum, and it takes months for the septum to thin and the outflow obstruction to ease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy still has disabling breathlessness despite a beta blocker and later verapamil. Echocardiogram shows systolic anterior motion (the anterior mitral leaflet pulled into the outflow tract) with mitral regurgitation. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker alone","Long-term nitrate therapy","Escalating loop diuretics","Surgical septal myectomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Surgical myectomy excises part of the septal myocardium causing outflow obstruction, and the mitral regurgitation from systolic anterior motion may resolve with myectomy alone, without valve replacement. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has new palpitations. Her pulse is irregularly irregular and the electrocardiogram shows atrial fibrillation. Echocardiogram shows left atrial enlargement. Which treatment prevents embolic stroke in her?","choices":["Anticoagulation","Digoxin for rate control","Nitrate therapy","Diuretic therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is treated with anticoagulation to prevent embolic stroke, together with antiarrhythmics such as amiodarone and, where needed, radiofrequency ablation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is diagnosed with dilated cardiomyopathy. His father had a cardiomyopathy and his older brother died suddenly at 35. No alcohol, drug or chemotherapy exposure is found. Which is the most appropriate referral for him and his family?","choices":["Family screening only after age 60","Genetic counseling for the family","Reassurance that it is not inherited","Heart biopsies for his relatives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Familial dilated cardiomyopathy accounts for up to 35% of cases, usually autosomal dominant, and genetic counseling is part of patient education when there is a family history of cardiomyopathy or sudden death. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with cardiomyopathy has become lightheaded on standing since his medications were increased at his last visit. He is frail and has lost weight. Which parts of his drug therapy call for particular caution at his age?","choices":["Diuretics and vasodilators","Sodium restriction advice","Home-based rehabilitation","Implanted device checks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age-specific strategies for elderly patients with cardiomyopathy call for cautious use of diuretics and vasodilators, with therapy tailored to comorbidity and frailty and a focus on symptom control. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man who has drunk heavily for 20 years has six months of worsening breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). The apical impulse is displaced laterally and a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound just after the second heart sound) is heard. Echocardiogram shows dilated, thin-walled ventricles with an ejection fraction of 25%, and coronary angiography shows no significant narrowing. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stress cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dilation and impaired contraction without severe coronary disease or pressure or volume overload define dilated cardiomyopathy, and long-term excess alcohol is one of its named causes. The displaced apical impulse and third heart sound fit a dilated, volume-overloaded ventricle. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
